From a3292ee250b84880c51e2f24cf757018d0197ef6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Edison Flores <eddyflores100-lang@protonmail.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 02:18:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Tspan.shape_box: subtract fontsize so the box covers where
 the glyphs render
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Bug #634.

In SVG, the y coordinate of a <text>/<tspan> element is the baseline
position. The visible glyphs render *above* that baseline — the
font's ascender extends upward from y. The previous implementation of
Tspan.shape_box() added the fontsize to y, which placed the entire
bounding box below the anchor point — the opposite of where the
visible text actually appears.

For the SVG snippet from the bug report:

    <text x="1.3443393" y="69.905418">
        <tspan x="1.3443393" y="69.905418"
               style="font-size:85.3333px">XXX</tspan>
    </text>

the previous code computed the bounding box as
  x in [1.34, 1.34], y in [69.91, 155.24]
which is entirely below the visible glyphs. After this fix it
computes
  x in [1.34, 1.34], y in [-15.43, 69.91]
which actually contains the rendered text.

Subtract instead of add. No behaviour change for callers that only
used shape_box() as a coarse approximation (the docstring still
warns the box is "horrible" — only its vertical span changes sign).
---
 inkex/elements/_text.py | 9 ++++++++-
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/inkex/elements/_text.py b/inkex/elements/_text.py
index 3c96836..af5cfb1 100644
--- a/inkex/elements/_text.py
+++ b/inkex/elements/_text.py
@@ -220,7 +220,14 @@ class Tspan(ShapeElement, TextBBMixin):
         x1, y1 = effective_transform.apply_to_point((self.x, self.y))
         fontsize = self.to_dimensionless(self.style.get("font-size", "12px"))
         x2 = self.x + 0  # XXX This is impossible to calculate!
-        y2 = self.y + float(fontsize)
+        # In SVG, the y coordinate of a text/tspan element is the baseline
+        # position, and the glyphs render *above* that baseline (the font's
+        # ascender extends upwards from y). The previous implementation added
+        # the fontsize to y, which placed the bounding box entirely below the
+        # anchor point - the opposite of where the visible glyphs actually
+        # are. Subtract instead so the box covers the area above the baseline
+        # where the text is drawn.
+        y2 = self.y - float(fontsize)
         x2, y2 = effective_transform.apply_to_point((x2, y2))
         return BoundingBox((x1, x2), (y1, y2))
 
-- 
